"This surah begins by declaring that everything in the heavens and on earth glorifies Allah. Allah is al-Aziz (the Almighty), meaning the Absolute Victor and Prevalent; He grants honor to whomever He wills and abases whomever He wills. He is also al-Hakim (the All-Wise), meaning that He does everything with wisdom. This introduction is in harmony with the content of the surah: for the event of the expulsion of the Jews of Banu Nadir, which is to be recounted, is a manifestation of Allah's might and wisdom, and it is emphasized that there was no injustice or wrongdoing in this incident, but rather a purification.\n\nAllah's might and wisdom expelled from their homes those who disbelieved among the People of the Book—namely, the tribe of Banu Nadir. They had come to await the final Prophet, yet when he arrived, they disbelieved. This expulsion is called the 'first gathering' (a minor resurrection) and is an example of the great gathering in the Hereafter. Neither the believers nor the disbelievers anticipated that they would be expelled; they relied upon their fortresses and their strength. But Allah defeated them from where they did not anticipate, by casting terror into their hearts, so much so that they began to destroy their homes with their own hands. This event is a great lesson for those who have eyes to see: the consequence of disbelief, oppression, and relying solely on material causes is bitter; whereas faith and sincere reliance on Allah bring His help.\n\nAccording to the legal theorists (usul scholars), the command 'take admonition' in the ayah indicates that analogical reasoning (qiyas) is a valid proof. Taking admonition (ibrah) is the transition from an observed situation to understanding an unobserved one. Here, proceeding from the punishment of Banu Nadir because of their oppression and disbelief, the conclusion is drawn that every act of oppression and disbelief certainly merits punishment. This punishment may be in this world or in the Hereafter; by Allah's wisdom, it is sometimes deferred and sometimes inflicted immediately. From this perspective, analogical reasoning is valid.\n\nHad Allah not decreed exile (jala') for them, He would have inflicted upon them a greater punishment in this world. Jala' means the forcible expulsion of a community from their homeland. Banu Nadir were punished with exile, whereas Banu Qurayzah, who took no admonition, were killed. In either case, the punishment of the Fire awaits them in the Hereafter. Even if they escape worldly punishment, they cannot escape the punishment of the Hereafter.\n\nRegarding whether or not to cut down date palms during war, it is stated: 'Whatever date palms you cut down or left standing on their trunks, it was by Allah's permission.' This permission was granted in order to disgrace those transgressors. The word 'linah' mentioned in the ayah means date palm; some scholars have stated that it refers to the most prized kind of date palm. This dispensation was granted for the purpose of weakening the enemy.\n\nThe ruling regarding the spoils acquired from Banu Nadir is different. Allah granted these properties to His Messenger as fay'. Fay' refers to spoils obtained without fighting and without spurring horses. These were properties for which the Muslims spurred neither horses nor camels; therefore, they belonged solely to the Messenger of Allah. He, in turn, spent them in the cause of Allah after setting aside a year's maintenance for his family. Ganimah (war booty), on the other hand, is obtained through fighting, and its ruling is subject to the one-fifth division explained in Surah al-Anfal. Fay' is broader than ganimah; it also encompasses revenues such as land tax (kharaj) and poll tax (jizyah).\n\nIn the subsequent ayah, a general ruling is declared with the expression 'the spoils that Allah granted to His Messenger from the people of the towns.' This encompasses all revenues obtained from conquered cities. After the conquest of the lands of Iraq, Umar cited these verses as evidence to argue that the lands should not be distributed among the Muslims but should instead be retained as kharaj-bearing lands, and the Companions adopted this view. Thus, future generations were also able to benefit from these revenues.\n\nThis ayah indicates that the spoils are divided into six shares: Allah, the Prophet, near relatives, orphans, the needy, and wayfarers. Some scholars made it six shares, allocating Allah's share to be spent on the repair of the Kaaba and mosques. According to another view, it is divided into five shares by counting a single share for Allah and His Messenger. Although it bears similarity to the verse in Surah al-Anfal, the stipulation of one-fifth is not explicitly mentioned here.\n\nThere are different views regarding the share of the Messenger of Allah. According to the Hanafis, this share ceased after the demise of the Prophet, because the Rightly Guided Caliphs acted in this manner. According to one view of al-Shafi'i, this share is given to the imam (leader), because the Prophet took this share in his capacity as head of state. The majority of the Shafi'is hold that this share must be spent on matters that serve the benefit of the Muslims.\n\nWhat is meant by 'near relatives' (dhu al-qurba) is the Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib. The Prophet did not allocate a share from the spoils to relatives outside these two clans. This matter is explained in a hadith: the Prophet considered Banu al-Muttalib as part of his own family, as they had aided him during the pre-Islamic era of ignorance (Jahiliyyah) as well. The Hanafis stated that this share is to be given only to needy relatives, not to the wealthy.\n\nAn orphan is a poor child whose father has passed away; the destitute (miskin) is an impoverished person who possesses nothing; and the wayfarer (ibn al-sabil) is a traveler stranded on the road. These categories must be both Muslim and poor. There are differences of opinion among scholars regarding the definitions of the poor (faqir) and the destitute (miskin). According to the Hanafis, the poor is one who does not possess the nisab threshold of wealth, whereas the destitute is one who has nothing at all.\n\nThe wisdom behind distributing the spoils in this manner is so that wealth does not become a commodity circulating only among the rich. The word 'dulah' mentioned in the ayah signifies wealth and bounty. It is commanded to take whatever the Prophet gives, to refrain from whatever he forbids, and to fear Allah. The distribution of the spoils was left entirely to the Prophet; obedience to those in authority (ulu al-amr) also falls within this scope.\n\nAlthough this command was revealed specifically concerning the spoils, as the exegetes have pointed out, its meaning encompasses all commands. In a hadith narrated by al-Bukhari, Muslim, Abu Dawud, and al-Tirmidhi, Ibn Mas'ud stated that Allah has cursed women who practice tattooing and those who have it done, those who pluck facial hair, those who file their teeth for beauty, and those who alter Allah's creation. When a woman named Umm Ya'qub claimed that this was not in the Quran, Ibn Mas'ud reminded her of the verse: 'Whatever the Messenger gives you, take it; and whatever he forbids you, abstain from it.' This demonstrates that what the Messenger of Allah forbids is also regarded as Allah's command.\n\nIt is narrated from Imam al-Shafi'i that he said he could answer every question posed regarding the Book of Allah and the Sunnah of the Messenger; he then stated that Umar's order to kill hornets (zanbur) was binding because the Messenger of Allah commanded obedience to Umar, and the Book of Allah commanded obedience to the command of the Messenger of Allah. This is not an analogy in the form of 'The order of the commander is the order of the commander of the commander's commander,' but rather drawing a deduction directly from the supreme proof. Obedience to those in authority who do not contradict Allah and His Messenger is also declared in verse 59 of Surah an-Nisa.\n\nAs stated in the works of jurisprudence (fiqh), if the head of state so chooses, he may divide a territory conquered by force ('anwatan) among the Muslims and impose the tithe ('ushr) upon it, as the Messenger of Allah did at Khaybar, or he may leave the inhabitants in place and collect jizyah and kharaj, as Umar did in the lands of Iraq. According to the Hanafis, this fay' (jizyah and kharaj) is not divided into five; the entirety of it is spent for the benefit of the Muslims, such as on soldiers, workers, judges, and imams. In the distribution of the portion of the spoils other than the one-fifth, the Messenger of Allah and the head of state likewise possess the discretion to choose through legal reasoning (ijtihad) that takes into account the needs of the Muslims.\n\nIn the eighth ayah, it is stated that the spoils are for the poor Emigrants (Muhajirun). These are the ones who were expelled from their homes and wealth, seeking Allah's bounty and pleasure, and aiding Allah and His Messenger. It is they who are the truthful—that is, upright and faithful people who prove their words through their deeds. According to Qatadah's narration, the Emigrants suffered such hardships that some tied stones to their stomachs out of hunger, while others slept in pits without bedding. They abandoned worldly pleasures, persevered for the sake of religion, and strived in jihad.\n\nThe ninth ayah is about the Ansar (Helpers). Before the Emigrants, they prepared Medina, embraced faith, loved those who emigrated, and felt no longing or covetousness in their hearts for what was given to them. Even if they themselves were in need, they gave preference to the Emigrants over themselves (îsâr). This is the highest station of morality. The Messenger of Allah distributed the spoils of Banu Nadir among the Emigrants, giving only to three needy persons among the Ansar, and they accepted it with pleasure. Furthermore, in a narration reported from Abu Hurayrah, a man from the Ansar went without the meal meant for his own daughter and stayed hungry in the dark in order to host the guest of the Messenger of Allah.\n\nThe word 'shuhh' mentioned in the ayah refers to the soul's disposition toward jealousy and stinginess. Ibn Mas'ud defined shuhh as 'unjustly consuming one's brother's wealth,' whereas Ibn Umar described it as 'coveting what one does not possess.' Being protected from stinginess is a condition for attaining salvation. Many hadiths emphasize the evil of stinginess. For example, it was stated: 'Nothing ruins Islam other than stinginess.' However, being protected from stinginess does not require being generous in everything; paying zakat, hosting guests, and helping in times of need are sufficient. Ali also said, 'Whoever pays the zakat on his wealth has been protected from the stinginess of his soul.'\n\nThe tenth ayah describes those who came after the Emigrants and the Ansar. These are the Successors (Tabi'un) and all believers who follow them in good conduct. This ayah shows that they, too, are partners with the earlier ones in the ruling on the spoils. Umar stated that the ayah encompasses all Muslims, saying, 'If I live, the share of the shepherd tending his flock will reach him without breaking a sweat.' Moreover, Umar acted prudently in the division of conquered lands with future generations in mind.\n\nThe other half of Khaybar was set aside for future hardships that might arise and matters such as visiting envoys. This portion was conceived as a fund for unforeseen emergencies that Muslims might encounter. The Messenger of Allah leased these lands to the Jews in return for half of the produce; this arrangement was in the form of musâkât (sharecropping of orchards in exchange for fruit) and müzârea (sharecropping of farmland). This practice continued during the caliphate of Abu Bakr. When Umar became caliph, as the number of Muslim laborers increased, the Jews were expelled to Syria (Sham) and the lands were distributed among Muslims. This practice of Umar shows that it is not obligatory to partition lands conquered by force of arms; indeed, Mecca was conquered by force, yet it was not partitioned. Imam Malik held the view that lands taken entirely through conquest should be placed in endowment (waqf) on behalf of the Muslims.\n\nUmar did not divide the lands of Iraq (Sawad) as war spoils, but left them in the hands of the local inhabitants, imposing on them kharaj (land tax) and jizya (poll tax). In doing so, he cited verses 7–10 of Surah al-Hashr as evidence, maintaining that these verses command the preservation of the rights of all Muslims, including future generations. Although some of the Companions, such as al-Zubayr, Bilal, and Salman, initially favored division, they too accepted this view following Umar's lengthy address and his substantiation with verses from the Quran. Thus, the lands of Iraq were left to the local population in return for kharaj and jizya: for every dunam, one dirham and one qafiz; for vineyards, ten dirhams; for clover fields, five dirhams of kharaj; as for individuals, a jizya of 48 dirhams for the wealthy, 24 for those of average means, and 12 for the poor was assessed. Eighty million dirhams were collected in the first year, and one hundred and twenty million in the second year.\n\nImam al-Ghazali states in his work al-Ihya: It is not permissible to give wealth from the public treasury (bayt al-mal) to the wealthy who are of no benefit to the public. Although scholars have differed on this matter, this is what is correct. Even though Umar's practice indicates that every Muslim has a right to the public treasury, he distributed wealth only to those possessing specific attributes. Accordingly, it is permissible, and indeed necessary, to provide a sufficient stipend to those who work for the benefit of Muslims and are occupied with earning their livelihood (such as scholars, teachers, muezzins, students of knowledge, administrators, soldiers, scribes, and financial officials). They may be paid even if they are wealthy, for the Rightly Guided Caliphs gave even to those who were not in need. The amount depends on the discretion (ijtihad) of the head of state; depending on the situation, an amount is given that will make them wealthy or suffice them.\n\nThe 10th ayah does not use an absolute expression like 'those who came after'; rather, it describes their state as follows: \"Our Lord, forgive us and our brothers who preceded us in faith, and leave no rancor in our hearts toward those who have believed. Our Lord, You are indeed Kind and Merciful.\" This ayah indicates that Muslims must not harbor rancor toward one another, must respect their elders, and must pray for the forgiveness of shortcomings. Ibn Umar heard a man reviling the Emigrants and recited to him verses 8, 9, and 10 in succession; when the man reached the last verse and asked, \"Am I among these?\", he replied, \"No, whoever reviles them cannot be among them.\" Citing this ayah as evidence, Imam Malik also said that whoever reviles or hates one of the Companions has no share in the spoils.\n\nTurning to the condition of the hypocrites: In the 11th ayah, the promises made by the hypocrites to their disbelieving friends among the People of the Book (the Jews of Banu Nadir) are described. Hypocrites such as Abdullah ibn Ubayy told the Jews, \"If you are expelled, we will surely leave with you; we will never obey anyone against you; and if you are fought against, we will help you.\" Allah bears witness that they are liars. In the 12th ayah, it is reported that these promises will not be kept: If they are expelled, they will not leave with them, and if they are fought against, they will not help them; even if they were to help them, they would turn their backs and flee, and they themselves will not be helped. The 13th ayah states that they fear you in their hearts more than they fear Allah, because they are a people who do not understand.\n\nIn the 14th ayah, it is stated that they cannot fight you united except in fortified towns or from behind walls; that there is intense discord among themselves; and that although they appear united, their hearts are divided. This is because they are a people who do not use their intellect. The 15th ayah likens them to the polytheists who met their punishment at Badr, or to the Jews of Banu Qaynuqa, who tasted the evil consequences of their deeds. The story of Banu Qaynuqa is related: they broke the treaty, exposed the private parts of a woman, then retreated into their fortresses, and eventually surrendered and were expelled. In verses 16–17, the state of the hypocrites is likened to Satan: he first says to man, \"Disbelieve,\" but once man disbelieves, he says, \"I am free of you; indeed, I fear Allah.\" In the end, both are in the Fire eternally. This is the recompense of all wrongdoers. Both the one who commands and the one who commits the act are responsible; and deeming wrongdoing permissible is disbelief.\n\nIn verse 18, the believers are addressed: \"Fear Allah, and let every soul look to what it has prepared for tomorrow; indeed, Allah is aware of what you do.\" In verse 19, it is commanded not to be like those who forgot Allah, and so Allah made them forget themselves; they are the defiantly disobedient (fasiqun). In verse 20, it is emphasized that the companions of the Fire and the companions of Paradise are not equal, and that the companions of Paradise are the attainers of their desires. In verse 21, the majesty of the Quran is described: Had it been sent down upon a mountain, it would have humbled itself and split apart out of fear of Allah. These parables are given so that people may reflect.\n\nIn verses 22–24, Allah's beautiful names and attributes are enumerated: He is the One besides Whom there is no deity, the Knower of the unseen and the witnessed, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. He is the Sovereign, the Pure, the Source of Peace, the Granter of Security, the Guardian, the Almighty, the Compeller, the Supremely Great; the Creator, the Originator, the Fashioner. To Him belong the most beautiful names. Whatever is in the heavens and the earth glorifies Him. He is the Almighty, the All-Wise. These verses conclude the surah with a magnificent proclamation of tawhid and glorification.\n\nIn these verses, it is indicated that the life of this world is one day and the life of the Hereafter is one day. It means 'today is the worldly life, tomorrow is the Hereafter.' Man must consider every day what he has prepared for tomorrow. Although the command 'Fear Allah!' may appear to reinforce the preceding command, the former expresses fulfilling commands out of love for Allah, whereas this one expresses avoiding prohibitions out of fear of Allah. Allah is aware of everything you do and will grant punishment or reward tomorrow accordingly.\n\nIn verse 19, we are commanded not to resemble those who forgot Allah, and so Allah made them forget themselves. These people do not know what they are doing, do not understand human worth, and do not contemplate good and virtue. On the Day of Resurrection, they are subjected to terrible tragedies. Even those who claim there is no soul are such self-forgetting people. The innate nature (fitrah) of those heedless of consciousness has become corrupted. They are indeed the defiantly disobedient (fasiqun)—a corrupted group that has departed from obedience and plunged into rebellion. Believers must not resemble them.\n\nIn verse 20, it is emphasized that the companions of the Fire and the companions of Paradise are not equal. The transgressors who abandoned Allah and indulged in sin deserved Hell, while those who feared Allah and protected themselves became worthy of Paradise. Those who attain salvation are only the people of Paradise. To avert this danger and attain this great goal, believers must protect themselves from sins, reckon every day what they have prepared for tomorrow, and not forget Allah and become like the transgressors.\n\nIn verse 21, it is explained that had the Quran been sent down upon a mountain, that mountain would have bowed down and cleaved asunder out of fear of Allah. It is recalled that the mountains did not accept the trust; the Quran was revealed to human beings. While people ought to be even more deeply moved by this impact, most unjust and ignorant people are not affected by it. These parables are given so that people may reflect and take heed. Reflection (fikr) consists of arranging pieces of knowledge in order and connecting the conclusion to what preceded it.\n\nIn verses 22–23, the attributes of Allah are described: There is no deity except Him; He knows the unseen and the witnessed; He is the Most Gracious (al-Rahman) and the Most Merciful (al-Rahim). He is al-Malik, al-Quddus, al-Salam, al-Mu'min, al-Muhaymin, al-Aziz, al-Jabbar, al-Mutakabbir. He is far exalted above the partners that the polytheists associate with Him. The attributes al-Rahman and al-Rahim express two kinds of mercy: the Rahmaniyyah encompasses everyone, whereas the Rahimiyyah is granted to those who strive. These verses instill fear and hope together.\n\nIn verse 24, it is stated that Allah is al-Khaliq, al-Bari, and al-Musawwir. To Him belong the most beautiful names. Creation (khalq) means determining and bringing into existence; al-Bari is the One Who creates purely and soundly; al-Musawwir is the One Who shapes forms as He wills. Attributes are divided into three: essential (dhatiyyah), active (fi'liyyah), and qualitative (ma'nawiyyah). The names of Allah are ninety-nine; whoever enumerates them will enter Paradise. These names should be invoked with reverence, mentioning good and evil together.\n\nAccording to what al-Jami' al-Saghir records, the names of Allah Almighty established in the Book and the Sunnah explicitly or by allusive indication are not limited to ninety-nine. In the Quran, there are many more names and attributes such as Rabbu'l-alamin, 'alimu'l-ghaybi wash-shahadah, and ghafiru'dh-dhanb. These are of such nature that they cannot be attributed to any other than Allah. Likewise, names mentioned in supplications in al-Bukhari and Muslim, such as munzilu'l-kitab and mujri's-sahab, belong exclusively to Allah.\n\nIn a hadith narrated from Abdullah b. Mas'ud, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'If any Muslim is afflicted with distress or grief and says: \"O Allah, I am Your servant, the son of Your servant, and the son of Your maidservant. My forelock is in Your hand. Your judgment over me is prevailing, and Your decree concerning me is just. I ask You by every name that belongs to You, which You have named Yourself with, or revealed in Your Book, or taught to any of Your creation, or preserved in the knowledge of the unseen with You, that You make the Quran the spring of my heart, the polish of my sorrow, and the dispeller of my grief and anxiety,\" Allah Almighty will dispel his distress and grant him relief in place of his grief.'\n\nIn the hadith narrated by al-Bayhaqi from Aisha, Aisha wanted to learn from the Messenger of Allah the name of Allah through which supplications are accepted, and the Messenger of Allah told her to perform ablution, pray two rak'ahs, and then make supplication. In her supplication, Aisha said: 'O Allah, I ask You by all Your beautiful names, those we know and those we do not know, and by Your supreme name, with which when someone supplicates You, You are pleased, and with which when one asks You, You grant,' and the Messenger of Allah said: 'You have struck the truth.'\n\nThe statement in the previous hadith, 'Allah has ninety-nine names; whoever enumerates them will enter Paradise,' does not mean that the names are limited to ninety-nine. This indicates that whoever enumerates ninety-nine of the known most beautiful names will enter Paradise. The fact that an even one hundred was not counted and one was excluded is to show that observing an odd number is recommended (mustahabb). Indeed, the fact that in the glorification prayers (tasbih) 'Subhan Allah', 'al-hamdulillah', and 'Allahu Akbar' are recited thirty-three times each, totaling ninety-nine, is based on this wisdom.\n\nThere are various narrations regarding the virtue of the final verses of Surah al-Hashr. In the hadith narrated from Ma'qil b. Yasar, it is reported that whoever says 'A'udhu billahi minash-shaytanir-rajim' three times in the morning and recites the last three verses of Surah al-Hashr, seventy thousand angels will invoke blessings upon him, and if he dies on that day, he will die as a martyr. In al-Daylami's narration, it is stated that Allah's greatest names are in the last six verses of Surah al-Hashr. Moreover, it is narrated that these verses are a cure for headache, and that Gabriel (Jibril), when bringing down this verse, said: 'Place your hand upon your head.'\n\nHowever, the purpose of these verses is not merely reciting them or practicing incantations, but placing one's hand on one's head and reflecting, being filled with the fear of Allah, and preparing for tomorrow in accordance with the verse 'O mankind, fear Allah, and let a person look to what he has sent forth for tomorrow.' One must fear offending the majesty of Allah, work for the future with faith and piety (taqwa) by glorifying and sanctifying Him, and attain the salvation heralded by His mercy. O my Allah, the Almighty, the All-Wise, the Compassionate, and the Most Merciful! Enable us to attain our goal through Your forgiveness, leave no malice in our hearts toward our believing brothers, and increase our faith and love."